1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,690
>> Imagine a party with 23 guests.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,340
>> What are the chances that two of them share a birthday?

3
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:11,860
>> With 365 days to choose from, a match sounds pretty unlikely.

4
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:15,750
>> And yet it's more likely than not. The chance is 50.7%.

5
00:00:15,830 --> 00:00:19,590
>> That's better than a coin toss, with just 23 people?

6
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:25,730
>> Yes. And with 70 guests, it's 99.9%, which is almost certain.

7
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:31,030
>> This is the birthday problem. Let's find out why it works.

8
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:38,570
>> Let's set some rules for our party first.

9
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:46,520
>> Each birthday is one of 365 days, all equally likely, and we'll leave out February 29.

10
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:51,950
>> Now the guests arrive one at a time, and we mark each birthday on the calendar.

11
00:00:52,030 --> 00:00:55,750
>> So far, every guest has a day to themselves.

12
00:00:55,830 --> 00:01:03,220
Until guest 20 lands on a day that's already taken. It's November 19, the birthday of guest 7.

13
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:05,930
>> Twenty guests, and we already have a match.

14
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:12,270
That's just one party, though. So here are 1,000 more, each with 23 random guests.

15
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:18,910
>> 511 of them have a shared birthday. That's about half, just as the maths says.

16
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:26,220
>> That's far fewer guests than our instincts suggest. In one study, most people markedly overestimated the number needed.

17
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:28,290
>> So who first noticed this?

18
00:01:28,370 --> 00:01:33,110
>> It's usually credited to the mathematician Harold Davenport, around 1927.

19
00:01:33,190 --> 00:01:39,650
>> But he didn't publish it, or claim it as his own, because he couldn't believe nobody had stated it before.

20
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:44,900
>> The first published version came from Richard von Mises, in 1939.

21
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,290
>> So why does our intuition get this so wrong?

22
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:53,920
>> It's natural to think about your own birthday, and whether anyone matches it.

23
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,420
>> Right, and with 22 other guests, the chance that someone shares yours is only about 6%.

24
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:04,220
>> Six percent? That's tiny.

25
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,520
>> But any two guests can make a match, not just you and someone else.

26
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:12,680
>> So let's draw a line between every pair of guests.

27
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:18,270
>> The first guest pairs with 22 others, the next with 21 more, and so on.

28
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:20,080
>> The lines just keep coming.

29
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:25,830
That's 23 × 22 ÷ 2, which makes 253 pairs.

30
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:30,780
>> So that's 253 chances for a match, not 22.

31
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:37,340
>> Exactly. Only 22 of those pairs include you. The other 231 are matches you weren't counting.

32
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:46,380
>> And pairs grow fast. Double the guests to 46, and there are 1,035 pairs, which is about four times as many.

33
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:51,290
>> So pairs grow like the square of the guests. That will matter later.

34
00:02:53,890 --> 00:02:59,790
For the exact chance, it's easier to work out the opposite, which is that no two guests share a birthday.

35
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:05,110
>> That's the chance that everyone is different. Then the rest is the chance of a match.

36
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:12,560
So, the first guest can have any birthday, which gives 365 days out of 365.

37
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:17,660
>> The second must miss the first, so it's 364 out of 365.

38
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:24,900
>> The third must miss both, which leaves 363 out of 365, and so on.

39
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:32,860
>> And guest 23 must miss all 22 before, which leaves 343 out of 365.

40
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:40,260
>> Multiply all 23 fractions, and the chance that every birthday differs is 49.3%.

41
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:44,100
>> So the chance of at least one shared birthday is 50.7%.

42
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:47,590
>> Every one of those fractions looks so harmless, though.

43
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:52,760
>> Each one is close to 1, but there are many, and each new guest has more birthdays to avoid.

44
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:57,730
>> Let's do the same sum for every size of party, and watch the chance climb.

45
00:03:57,810 --> 00:04:06,530
>> With 10 guests, the chance is 11.7%; with 30, it's 70.6%; with 50, it's 97%.

46
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:13,880
>> It passes 99% at 57 guests, and 99.9% at 70.

47
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:16,360
>> So when is a match actually guaranteed?

48
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:22,760
>> It's only at 366 guests, when there are more guests than days.

49
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:32,900
Let's watch it happen. Guests enter a room one by one, and we stop at the first shared birthday.

50
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:36,260
>> In this room, the first match came with guest 28.

51
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:41,400
>> In this one, it came with guest 33, and in this one, with guest 18.

52
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:44,140
>> It really does vary from room to room.

53
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:52,090
>> So let's run a thousand rooms. Each dot is one room, placed at the guest who brought its first match.

54
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:57,330
>> A few rooms matched very early, with just 3 guests. A few went past 60.

55
00:04:57,410 --> 00:05:03,550
>> In 511 of the 1,000 rooms, the first match came by guest 23.

56
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:05,020
>> Just over half, then.

57
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:12,840
>> And on average, the first match took 24.7 guests. The theory says about 24.6.

58
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:19,360
>> Real groups behave the same way. At the 2014 World Cup, each of the 32 squads had 23 players.

59
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:23,030
>> So every squad was one of our rooms of 23.

60
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:30,500
>> And an analysis of the squad lists suggested that 16 of them had a shared birthday, which is exactly half.

61
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:34,650
>> Five of those squads even had two pairs.

62
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:43,060
>> Now let's change the question. How many people do you need for an even chance that someone shares your birthday?

63
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:48,280
>> Surely it's half of 365, so about 183?

64
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:53,140
>> Let's see. Each person matches you with a chance of 1 in 365.

65
00:05:53,220 --> 00:06:01,710
>> So the chance that nobody does is 364 out of 365, multiplied once for every person.

66
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:07,960
>> That shrinks slowly. It takes 253 people to reach an even chance.

67
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:13,730
>> That's far more than half of 365. Where do all the extra people go?

68
00:06:13,810 --> 00:06:17,660
>> Many of them share birthdays with each other, so they don't cover any new days.

69
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:26,380
>> Drop 253 random birthdays on the calendar, and they land on only 180 different days.

70
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:30,620
>> So your birthday has only about an even chance of being one of them.

71
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:37,690
>> Now look at that number again. 253 is exactly the number of pairs among 23 guests.

72
00:06:37,770 --> 00:06:39,080
>> Is that a coincidence?

73
00:06:39,160 --> 00:06:46,600
>> Not really. Both questions need about 253 chances, each of 1 in 365.

74
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:57,810
>> A 1-in-365 chance becomes an even bet after about 253 tries. That's 365 × ln 2.

75
00:06:58,510 --> 00:07:05,830
>> So the room of 23 and the crowd of 253 are really the same sum.

76
00:07:08,430 --> 00:07:14,120
And that gives us a quick rule for any number of days, not just 365.

77
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:17,470
>> With n guests, there are about n² ÷ 2 pairs.

78
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:22,320
>> It's like half of a square grid, with every guest against every other guest.

79
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:28,090
>> For an even chance we need about 253 pairs, so n² should be about 506.

80
00:07:28,170 --> 00:07:35,180
>> The square root of 506 is about 22.5. Round up, and you get 23.

81
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:40,560
>> In general, you need about 1.18 × √days.

82
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:43,360
>> So what about a year with a million days?

83
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:49,290
>> With a million equally likely days, just 1,178 people would give an even chance.

84
00:07:49,370 --> 00:07:53,260
>> Out of a million days? That's about a tenth of a percent.

85
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:58,910
>> The real surprise is that the people needed grow only like the square root of the days.

86
00:08:01,510 --> 00:08:10,710
Of course, our party made some simplifications. It had 365 equally likely days, no February 29, and no twins.

87
00:08:10,790 --> 00:08:18,730
>> And real birthdays aren't spread evenly. In the US, more babies tend to be born in September and October.

88
00:08:18,810 --> 00:08:29,790
>> From 1973 to 1999, September 16 was the most common US birthday, and Christmas Day the rarest, apart from February 29.

89
00:08:29,870 --> 00:08:35,830
>> So does that break the maths? No, any unevenness only makes a shared birthday more likely.

90
00:08:35,910 --> 00:08:38,250
>> More likely? Why's that?

91
00:08:38,330 --> 00:08:43,650
>> Take an extreme case, and imagine everyone was born in the first half of the year.

92
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:46,890
>> Then just 17 guests would give a better-than-even chance.

93
00:08:46,970 --> 00:08:51,880
>> There are fewer days to go round, so more people crowd onto the same ones.

94
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:56,300
>> Real birthdays are only slightly uneven, though, so the real-world answer is still 23.

95
00:08:56,380 --> 00:09:02,680
>> And twins nearly always share a birthday, which can only raise the chance.

96
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:09,000
The same maths matters in computer security, too.

97
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:14,090
>> A hash function turns any file into a short digital fingerprint of fixed length.

98
00:09:14,170 --> 00:09:19,680
>> A 64-bit fingerprint has about 18 billion billion possible values.

99
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:21,480
>> That sounds like plenty.

100
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:26,250
>> Still, two different files with the same fingerprint make a collision.

101
00:09:26,330 --> 00:09:31,100
>> And fingerprints are like birthdays, in a year with an enormous number of days.

102
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:38,370
>> So collisions show up after about the square root of that number. That's 2 to the power of half the bits.

103
00:09:38,450 --> 00:09:43,030
>> For 64 bits, about 5 billion tries give an even chance of a collision.

104
00:09:43,110 --> 00:09:46,080
>> And to match one particular fingerprint?

105
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:50,650
>> That's like matching your own birthday, and it takes about 13 billion billion tries.

106
00:09:50,730 --> 00:09:57,750
>> That's the idea behind the birthday attack. Imagine a forger with a fair contract and a fraudulent one.

107
00:09:57,830 --> 00:10:03,700
>> She makes many harmless variations of each, such as an extra comma, a double space or a synonym.

108
00:10:03,780 --> 00:10:09,900
>> When a fair version and a fraudulent version share a fingerprint, she has her collision.

109
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:15,190
>> Her victim signs the fair version, and the signature fits the fraudulent one just as well.

110
00:10:15,270 --> 00:10:18,380
>> That's sneaky. So how do you defend against it?

111
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:23,120
>> You use longer fingerprints, with about twice as many bits as brute force alone would demand.

112
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:36,070
>> SHA-256 has 256 bits, so a collision takes around 2 to the 128 tries. That's a 39-digit number.

113
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:42,050
>> So a party puzzle tells engineers how long their fingerprints need to be.

114
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:47,010
So, let's go back to the party.

115
00:10:47,090 --> 00:10:52,580
>> 23 guests sound like too few, because we picture matches with ourselves.

116
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:56,900
>> But every pair is a chance, and 23 guests make 253 pairs.

117
00:10:56,980 --> 00:11:01,990
>> That's enough for a better-than-even chance of 50.7%.

118
00:11:02,070 --> 00:11:05,590
>> It still feels wrong, doesn't it? Even when you can see every pair.

119
00:11:05,670 --> 00:11:10,460
>> That's what makes it a paradox. It seems wrong at first, but it's true.

120
00:11:10,540 --> 00:11:14,800
>> So next time you're in a room of 23 people or more, ask around.

121
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:19,600
>> More often than not, two of you will share a birthday.
